### What is the Longest Common Prefix problem?

The Longest Common Prefix problem finds the longest starting substring shared by every string in an array. If the input is ["flower", "flow", "flight"], the answer is "fl" because all three strings start with "fl" but disagree at the third character. This is a common interview problem that tests string traversal and edge-case handling.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LongestCommonPrefix {
    public static String longestCommonPrefix(String[] strs) {
        if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) return "";
        String first = strs[0];
        for (int i = 0; i < first.length(); i++) {
            char c = first.charAt(i);
            for (int j = 1; j < strs.length; j++) {
                if (i >= strs[j].length() || strs[j].charAt(i) != c) {
                    return first.substring(0, i);
                }
            }
        }
        return first;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

At the National Bank of India, account numbers within a branch share a common prefix that identifies the branch and product type. When Rajesh processes a batch of transfers, he extracts the longest common prefix to identify which branch the batch belongs to. This helps route the batch to the correct processing center without examining every individual account.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.dsa.LongestCommonPrefix;

public class BatchRoutingService {
    public String identifyBranch(String[] accountNumbers) {
        String prefix = LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(accountNumbers);
        System.out.println("Batch branch prefix: '" + prefix + "'");
        return prefix;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] batch = {"HDFC0012345", "HDFC0012890", "HDFC0012001"};
        new BatchRoutingService().identifyBranch(batch);
    }
}
```

```text
Batch branch prefix: 'HDFC0012'
```

### How does vertical scan work step by step?

Vertical scan treats the strings like rows of a grid and reads down each column. Take the first string as the reference. For column index i, read the character at position i from the reference string, then check that every other string has the same character at position i. If any string is shorter than position i or has a different character, return the reference substring from 0 to i. If you reach the end of the reference string without any mismatch, the entire reference is the common prefix.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class VerticalScanDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] accounts = {"ICICI001234", "ICICI001890", "ICICI002001"};
        String first = accounts[0];
        for (int i = 0; i < first.length(); i++) {
            char c = first.charAt(i);
            boolean allMatch = true;
            for (int j = 1; j < accounts.length; j++) {
                if (i >= accounts[j].length() || accounts[j].charAt(i) != c) {
                    allMatch = false;
                    break;
                }
            }
            System.out.println("Column " + i + " char='" + c + "' allMatch=" + allMatch);
            if (!allMatch) {
                System.out.println("Prefix: '" + first.substring(0, i) + "'");
                return;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Prefix: '" + first + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Column 0 char='I' allMatch=true
Column 1 char='C' allMatch=true
Column 2 char='I' allMatch=true
Column 3 char='C' allMatch=true
Column 4 char='I' allMatch=true
Column 5 char='0' allMatch=true
Column 6 char='0' allMatch=true
Column 7 char='1' allMatch=false
Prefix: 'ICICI00'
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time complexity is O(S) where S is the total number of character comparisons made. In the best case, when the first characters differ, only n comparisons are needed where n is the number of strings. In the worst case, when all strings are identical, S equals the total length of all strings. Space complexity is O(1) beyond the input, since no extra data structures are created.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ComplexityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Best case: first characters differ, only n comparisons
        String[] bestCase = {"ABC", "DEF", "GHI"};
        System.out.println(LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(bestCase)); // ""

        // Worst case: all identical, examines every character
        String[] worstCase = {"AAAA", "AAAA", "AAAA"};
        System.out.println(LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(worstCase)); // "AAAA"
    }
}
```

```text

AAAA
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty array returns an empty string. A single-element array returns that string itself because it is trivially the common prefix. If any string in the array is empty, the common prefix is immediately empty because no character can match position 0 of an empty string.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty array: '" + LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(new String[]{}) + "'");
        System.out.println("Single:      '" + LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(new String[]{"HDFC"}) + "'");
        System.out.println("Has empty:   '" + LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(new String[]{"HDFC", "", "HDFC"}) + "'");
        System.out.println("All same:    '" + LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(new String[]{"NBI", "NBI", "NBI"}) + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Empty array: ''
Single:      'HDFC'
Has empty:   ''
All same:    'NBI'
```

### How is vertical scan different from other approaches?

| Approach | How it works | Time | Space | Early exit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Vertical scan | Check column by column across all strings | O(S) | O(1) | Yes, at first mismatch |
| Horizontal scan | Fold prefix pairwise across strings | O(S) | O(m) | Less direct |
| Binary search | Binary search on prefix length, verify candidate | O(S log m) | O(1) | No |
| Trie | Insert all strings, walk single-child path | O(S) | O(S) | No |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class HorizontalScanDemo {
    // Horizontal scan: fold prefix pairwise
    public static String horizontalScan(String[] strs) {
        if (strs == null || strs.length == 0) return "";
        String prefix = strs[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < strs.length; i++) {
            while (!strs[i].startsWith(prefix)) {
                prefix = prefix.substring(0, prefix.length() - 1);
                if (prefix.isEmpty()) return "";
            }
        }
        return prefix;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] batch = {"HDFC0012345", "HDFC0012890", "HDFC0012001"};
        System.out.println("Horizontal: " + horizontalScan(batch));
        System.out.println("Vertical:   " + LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(batch));
    }
}
```

```text
Horizontal: HDFC0012
Vertical:   HDFC0012
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is not handling the empty array or null input, which causes an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. The second mistake is not checking whether a string is shorter than the current position before accessing its character, which causes a StringIndexOutOfBoundsException. The third mistake is using the wrong return value when the reference string is exhausted.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: no bounds check
    public static String buggyPrefix(String[] strs) {
        String first = strs[0]; // crashes on empty array
        for (int i = 0; i < first.length(); i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < strs.length; j++) {
                if (strs[j].charAt(i) != first.charAt(i)) { // crashes if strs[j] is shorter
                    return first.substring(0, i);
                }
            }
        }
        return first;
    }

    // CORRECT: guards null, empty, and length
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] safe = {"HDFC001", "HDFC", "HDFC00123"};
        System.out.println(LongestCommonPrefix.longestCommonPrefix(safe)); // "HDFC"
    }
}
```

```text
HDFC
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that vertical scan has the best early-exit behavior when prefixes are short, which is the common case for account numbers sharing a branch code. Horizontal scan is slightly less direct because it folds the prefix pairwise. Binary search on the prefix length has a log factor and is rarely needed. A trie is useful when you need to query the common prefix of different subsets repeatedly, but it uses O(S) extra space.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you find the longest common prefix of a subset of the array?
- Can you solve this with a Trie and what are the trade-offs?
- How would you adapt this for case-insensitive prefix matching?
- What if the input is a stream of strings arriving one at a time?
